ositions in the dna of the reference  organism high somatic scores correspond  to frequently observed mutations in  sequencing reads and we interpret them  to mean that these are mutations that  were acquired as a result of cell  division that's why they're called  somatic mutations  for example we can talk about  transcriptomic data we have a table of  continuous numeric data for gene isoform  or exon expression levels annotated by  gene id and organized consistently  across samples in a given experiment so  in this table you have  samples as columns gene ids as rows and  the intersection is the level of  expression of that gene for that sample  in the case of metagenomic data we look  at operational taxonomic unit so 16s  ribosomal rna amplicon which means that  these are only regions that are  amplified metagenomic sequencing data we  have a table of otus or operational  taxonomic units also across samples with  proportionate abundance in each  microbial community so together with  this table what we need is the  annotation of the taxonomy of these  operational taxonomic units to interpret  the composition of the microbiome sample  so to explore these tables we also learn  how to visualize different properties of  the data and summarize them using the  statistical properties that can help us  explore the major signal and variation  across samples  we also learned to use different  statistical approaches or analysis  techniques that help us find specific  patterns of interest such as for example  differentially expressed genes between  groups of samples  the statistical methods that we talk  about are  used widely today to find patterns that  are sort of easy to interpret  biologically so for example hypothesis  testing for each gene that represents  genes that are consistently and strongly  different between conditions so  differential gene expression analysis is  commonly understood to represent a  biologically meaningful way to identify  key genes that could be interpreted and  validated and so finally we spoke about  some of the major challenges because of  data variability and how the steps to  explore data are very important to  differentiate between signal and noise  specifically in the statistical  approaches we often times look for the  most significant patterns and then we  also have this challenge of minor  patterns that could actually describe  data that covers many different  conditions across a lot of variability  and in noisy data and that is when we  turn to machine learning and so machine  learning methods can be separated into  two main categories of methods  unsupervised machine learning that  focuses on detection  of patterns in data and the second main  category is the supervised machine  learning methods and these methods rely  on availability of training data so data  that has been labeled once a data set of  labeled data is accumulated it can be  used to train a model that will be able  to apply this knowledge to analyze new  data for example by predicting a class  so with unsupervised machine learning  methods we start with a data set  typically it's a table of elements just  like what we saw and here we can use  shapes as examples so we can use a  clustering method for example  hierarchical clustering or k-means  clustering and these could identify  groups in the data and the fact that it  places each sample into a group  based on some feature in this case shape  is the learning process and so the  outcome of the learning method is  identification and assignment of each  sample to a group which means that each  group has a boundary and we can study  each group separately  one unsupervised machine learning method  that we already talked about is  principal component analysis let's  imagine some kind of a data set that  looks like a cloud we can find a  particular type of a projection for  example we can take two of the first  features that can either show us low  variance or high variance now in either  case  maybe the features that we have in our  data don't represent the full data in a  very accurate way so what we can do is  we can have a projection  based on principal components that are  projected in this original data that  maximize variance or explain variance of  the original dataset  principle component analysis uses  explained variance to show us the most  accurate way to represent our data but  it is not always going to work well for  any kind of data in metagenomic  sequencing data we're interested to  understand how diversity changes across  samples and can be used to understand  composition which can be done using  non-metric multi-dimensional scaling or  nmds the goal of an mds is to collapse  information from multiple communities  sites etc into just a few so that they  can be visualized and compared unlike  other ordination techniques that rely on  primarily euclidean distances such as  principal coordinates analysis nmds uses  rank orders and thus it is an extremely  flexible technique that can accommodate  a variety of different kinds of data  another method that seeks to deal with  various assumptions about data  distribution and complex structure is t  sne or t sne which stands for t  distributed stochastic neighbor  embedding it uses connectivity to  establish neighborhoods and data and  then shows a lower dimension  representation of the connectedness  between data points using a projection  mapping that seeks to accurately  represent the original topology in our  data set perplexity is what t-sne would  use to represent the distance between  samples which is a measure for  information that is defined as 2 to the  power of the shannon entropy in t sne  the perplexity may be viewed as a knob  that sets the number of effective  nearest neighbors it is comparable with  the number of nearest neighbors k that  is employed in many manifold learners so  what we will do today is we will  continue our discussion about machine  learning and we will turn to clustering  cluster analysis or clustering is the  task of grouping a set of objects in  such a way that objects in the same  group are more similar to each other  than to those in other groups  importantly this approach tends to be  deterministic it seeks to determine for  each point which group to assign it to  while this is not the case for all  methods some determine belonging to a  group based on initiation which can lead  to different interpretations each run  this is the main difference between  simply representing the data in the  lower dimension and assigning an actual  cluster to each point  and this is specifically helpful when  some overlap between point groups make  it difficult to accurately determine  belonging to a group and provides a  rationale to study cluster points as a  part of a single unit so to explain  let's take a look at our data here we  have known groups that are clinical  subtypes of breast cancer each gene has  a profile so for example this is a  profile  of this particular gene this is a small  example so we only have 15 genes but if  we plot a bar plot for each gene we  start seeing some patterns and they show  some similarity between samples of the  same type some logic behind similarity  can now be determined by automatically  assigning each sample into a defined  group so that it belongs to just one  cluster using all of the gene expression  values so here we can see the patterns  but how do we use all of this data  together and actually come up with all  of the patterns determining the same  thing or belonging of samples to a  particular group there are many  different types of clustering and they  are defined by different clustering  algorithms they use several different  approaches so let's take a look at some  of these the partitioning algorithms  divide data objects into a number of  partitions where each partition  represents a cluster these clusters  should fulfill the following requirement  one each group must contain at least one  object and two each object must belong  to exactly one group for example we have  the k-means algorithm where the center  is the average of all points and  coordinates represent the arithmetic  mean there are many other partitioning  algorithms such as k modes pam clara  clarons and fcm the second example is  the hierarchical based where data is  organized in a hierarchical manner  depending on the medium of proximity  proximities are obtained by the  intermediate nodes a dendrogram  represents the data sets where  individual data is represented by leaf  nodes the initial cluster gradually  divides into several clusters as the  hierarchy continues hierarchical  clustering methods can be agglomerative  or bottom-up and divisive or top-down an  agglomerative clustering starts with one  object for each cluster and recursively  merges two or more of the most  appropriate clusters a divisive cluster  starts with the dataset as one cluster  and recursively splits the most  appropriate cluster the process  continues until a stopping criterion is  reached frequently the requested number  k of clusters the hierarchical method  has a major drawback though which  relates to the fact that once a step  merge or split is performed it cannot be  undone some examples for hierarchical  clustering include birch cure rock and  chameleon density based here data  objects are separated based on their  regions of density connectivity and  boundary they are closely related to the  point nearest neighbors a cluster  defined as a connected dense component  grows in any direction that density  leads to therefore density based  algorithms are capable of discovering  clusters of arbitrary shapes and also  this provides a natural protection  against outliers so the overall density  of a point is analyzed to determine the  functions of a data set that influence a  particular data point dbscan optics db  clast and then clue are algorithms that  use such a method to filter out noise  which are outlier and discover clusters  of arbitrary shapes another approach is  grid based where the space of the data  objects is divided into grids the main  advantage of this approach is that it  has fast processing time because it goes  through the data set once to compute the  statistical values of the grid the  accumulated grid data make grid-based  clustering techniques independent of the  number of data objects that employ a  uniform grid to collect regional  statistical data and then perform the  clustering on the grid instead of the  database directly the performance of a  grid-based method depends on the size of  the grid which is usually much less than  the size of the actual data set however  for highly regular data distributions  using a single uniform grid may not be  sufficient to obtain the required  clustering quality or fulfill the time  requirement some example methods include  wave cluster and sting that belong to  this category so finally we have the  model based approaches these are methods  that optimize the fit between the given  data and some predefined mathematical  model this is based on the assumption  that the data is generated by a mixture  of underlying probability distributions  and also it leads to a way of  automatically determining the number of  clusters based on standard statistics  taking noise which are the outliers into  account and thus yielding a robust  clustering method there are two major  approaches that are based on the model  based method statistical and neural  network approaches mklust is probably  the best known model based algorithm but  there are other good algorithms such as  em expectation maximization which uses a  mixture density model conceptual  clustering such as cobweb and neural  network approaches such as  self-organizing feature maps so let's  take a look at several examples for the  first three types of clustering methods  that we described so far  hierarchical clustering is one of the  most popular approaches because it  allows clustering on different levels  even though we typically choose and have  to specify the number of expected  clusters k the way this algorithm works  does not actually require the k but only  is needed at the end once the hierarchy  of data points is established to see how  let's demonstrate how this method works  so let's say we have our samples  organized in some space so imagine a pca  plot like this one 2d plot where each  sample is positioned in some perspective  that best represents the date of  variance the way the algorithm works is  it finds points in this space that are  the closest to each other and assigns  the center  for those two points as a pseudo cluster  we perform the same operation to find  the nearest  neighbor  and organize them together and so when  we connect those two points that's  called linkage and we use different  methods of measuring distance between  points to establish the link and  ultimately the algorithm will stop once  we reach all of the samples belonging to  the same cluster so this was an example  of bottom-up agglomerative clustering  let's see how this algorithm actually  work so here we have our points  distributed in this two-dimensional  space you can see that the nearest  points are organized as a single point  and once all of the points reach the  same cluster  now we know that we have built the full  hierarchy now based on this hierarchy we  can determine some kind of a threshold  where we cut off this hierarchical tree  and determine the number of samples in  each cluster to run clustering on the  tbinfo platform you will need to upload  the table of expression that we  generated when we analyze for example  our cell line data this data can be  downloaded directly from the lesson or  from a demo pipeline in the cell line  rna-seq demo clustering methods are  located under the data mining section of  the platform  hierarchical clustering is an example of  agglomerative or bottom-up clustering  method that starts with attributing each  sample to a separate cluster it then  finds the pair of the most similar  samples and unites them in one cluster  that is further distributed as one  quasi-sample this procedure is repeated  until coming to one cluster that  contains all of the samples so when we  put the data into the pipeline it will  load the data perform clustering and put  out the result in several different  formats that we will discuss in a little  bit so what are the parameters that we  have to choose first of all we have to  choose the right linkage which is a  parameter determines the definition for  measuring inter-cluster distance single  sets the definition as the distance  between the two nearest points in two  clusters whereas complete defines the  distance as that between the two  farthest points the two ward methods are  variation of ward's minimum variance  betting  the distance parameter is the second one  which specifies the distance metric to  use in measuring distance between data  points and this could be euclidean  manhattan maximum or canberra and then  we have the cluster's expected count  which is a parameter that sets the  cut-off height  of the hierarchical dendrogram to the  one that results in a given number of  clusters and then we have the option to  transpose our data which is a parameter  that allows us to transpose the table  and either analyze genes or analyze  samples so what is cluster linkage so  there are several different types we  talked about right and we talked about  the average linkage and this is the  method that finds all possible pairwise  distances for points belonging to two  different clusters and then calculates  the average this method is less affected  by outliers than the other ones  single linkage which uses the minimum  difference to determine clustering the  distance between two groups is defined  as the distance between their two  closest members and we have other types  like centroid and complete linkage then  we have different parameters for  distance and understanding the choice  between all of these different methods  really is based on your understanding of  the input data so the hierarchical  clustering algorithm is a process by  which a computer uses the input file  where columns or objects and rows are  features  then it assigns each object to separate  cluster or group and then it evaluates  all pairwise distances between clusters  using a chosen distance metric and  constructs a distance matrix  using the distance values then it finds  the pair of clusters with the shortest  distance and merges them into a single  cluster and then it repeats these steps  until we end up with a single cluster  for all of the samples and as a result  what do we get we get the hierarchy of  our samples  organized into a dendrogram here we have  an example of a dendrogram and you can  see that we can cut it off at different  points to get the desired number of  clusters at the very top we have two  clusters then we have four clusters then  we have eight clusters another method  that we will talk about is k-means which  is probably the most popular clustering  algorithm of all it is a partition type  of an approach the algorithm itself is  relatively simple starting with a  pre-specified number of cluster centers  each point is initially assigned to its  nearest center  in the next step for each segment the  centers are moved to the centroid of the  clustered points the points are then  reassigned to their nearest center the  process is repeated until moving the  centers derives little or no improvement  which is measured by the within cluster  sum of squares which is the total  squared distance between each point  and its clustered center  in the k-means pipeline we also input a  table of expression in a txt file and  after running the algorithm we're able  to observe three different things first  the k-means clustering does not do a  great job of clustering for a lot of  these examples because of the many  different features that we have  essentially thousands of features per  sample so if we compare the two methods  k means and the hierarchical clustering  we will see that they produce different  results so essentially because of the  metrics that we select we expect to find  the same number of clusters for example  four but depending on the metric that we  use we can see those clusters separating  and we can always evaluate the results  based on what we know about the samples  for example we know which sample belongs  to which clinical phenotype  now another type of clustering is  spectral clustering spectral clustering  techniques make use of the spectrum or  the eigenvalues of the similarity matrix  of the data to perform dimensionality  reduction before clustering in fewer  dimensions  the similarity matrix is provided as an  input and consists of a quantitative  assessment of the relative similarity of  each pair of points in the data set the  basic algorithm consists of several  steps that are based on the laplacian  matrix which is a way to represent a  graph of interconnected nodes and study  graph relationships the same approach is  used for non-linear embedding or  dimensionality reduction techniques like  umap  spectral clustering does not work well  on all data because it is  computationally expensive unless the  graph is sparse and the similarity  matrix can be efficiently constructed a  special case of spectral clustering is  called db scan which stands for density  based spatial clustering of applications  with noise  so now that we reviewed the methods and  some of the ways that we can run them  let's discuss when such methods can be  used and how to interpret the results or  analyze their outputs in a meaningful  way  for example we can compare how different  clustering and dimensionality reduction  technique can provide us with insights  about data so if we label our data based  on known labels like