s  across samples that can reveal  meaningful biological function that is  shared between groups of genes and  affected in similar ways through  perturbation so when we have different  conditions  we expect those conditions to affect  biological function so if we take our  data set and we separate it here in four  clusters and then we visualize  what are those patterns for each cluster  we can see that they are correlated now  if we find a group that is highly  correlated and different from other  patterns in the data we can then assume  perhaps they somehow are linked to each  other and maybe they form a biological  function so going through annotation as  you can see here on the bottom right can  help us understand what could be that  function okay so now that we reviewed  the methods and we understand how to run  them let's discuss how clustering  methods can be compared evaluated and  selected for a specific objective or job  one simple way to understand how well  your clustering works is to try to plot  a confusion matrix between known classes  if they are available and the cluster  assignment in this way you can see which  sample is not being assigned to the  proper category if the annotation is not  available the confusion matrix can be  used to see if the same sample is being  assigned to an inappropriate cluster or  to several different clusters and that  will result in maybe changing the  settings or maybe iterative runs of the  same algorithm  in some cases your challenge would be to  determine how many clusters to expect in  that case you can use metrics like  inertia for k-means or silhouette for a  variety of different methods inertia  measures how well a data set was  clustered by k means it is calculated by  measuring the distance between each data  point and its centroid squaring the  distance and summing these squares  across one cluster a good model is one  with low inertia and a low number of  clusters in addition to the confusion  matrix a hierarchical clustering method  can show a dendrogram of linkages  between samples which can be  interpretable and can improve your  understanding of data this can be done  visually or with a metric score like the  silhouette score  the silhouette score is a method that is  common for many types of clustering  methods the average silhouette method  computes the average  of observations for different values of  k the optimal number of clusters k is  the one that maximizes the average  silhouette over a range of possible  values for k  the silhouette coefficient is calculated  using the mean intracluster distance and  the mean nearest cluster distance for  each sample thus you can choose k  and iteratively calculate the score to  maximize nearest cluster distance the  silhouette ranges from minus one to plus  one where a high value indicates that  the object is well matched to its own  cluster and poorly matched to  neighboring clusters if most objects  have a high value then the clustering  configuration is appropriate if many  points have a low or negative value then  the clustering configuration may have  too many or too few clusters  the gap statistic is an approach that  can be applied to any clustering method  to evaluate how well we have assigned  the number k  so what it does is it compares the total  with intracluster variation for  different values of k with their  expected values under no reference  distribution of the data the estimate of  the optimal clusters will be value that  maximizes the gap statistic  which yields the largest gap statistic  so this means that the clustering  structure is far away from the random  uniform distribution of points which  means that you have chosen the correct  number of k  in many cases a visual representation of  data can help significantly to  understand why a certain group is  determined to be a cluster or why an  outlier can reduce the quality of  clustering combining your analysis with  a representation of data can be a